#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: sidebar_label
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli operations and observables"
msgstr "Mga Pauli na operasyon at observable"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{/* cspell:ignore tensoring */}\n"
msgstr "{/* cspell:ignore tensoring */}\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli matrices play a central role in the stabilizer formalism.\n"
"We'll begin the lesson with a discussion of Pauli matrices, including some of their basic algebraic properties, and we'll also discuss how Pauli matrices (and tensor products of Pauli matrices) can describe measurements.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga Pauli matrix ay may sentral na papel sa stabilizer formalism.\n"
"Sisimulan natin ang leksyon sa talakayan ng mga Pauli matrix, kasama na ang ilang pangunahing algebraic na katangian nito, at tatalakayin din natin kung paano maaaring ilarawan ng mga Pauli matrix (at tensor product ng mga Pauli matrix) ang mga sukat.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli operation basics {#pauli-operation-basics}"
msgstr "Mga pangunahing kaalaman sa Pauli operation {#pauli-operation-basics}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here are the Pauli matrices, including the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and "
"the three non-identity Pauli matrices.\n"
msgstr ""
"Narito ang mga Pauli matrix, kasama ang $2\\times 2$ identity matrix at ang "
"tatlong non-identity Pauli matrix.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathbb{I} =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  1 & 0\\\\\n"
"  0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"X =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  0 & 1\\\\\n"
"  1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"Y =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  0 & -i\\\\\n"
"  i & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"Z =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  1 & 0\\\\\n"
"  0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathbb{I} =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  1 & 0\\\\\n"
"  0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"X =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  0 & 1\\\\\n"
"  1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"Y =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  0 & -i\\\\\n"
"  i & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"Z =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  1 & 0\\\\\n"
"  0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Properties of Pauli matrices {#properties-of-pauli-matrices}"
msgstr "Mga katangian ng Pauli matrix {#properties-of-pauli-matrices}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All four of the Pauli matrices are both unitary and Hermitian.\n"
"We used the names $\\sigma_x,$ $\\sigma_y,$ and $\\sigma_z$ to refer to the non-identity Pauli matrices earlier in the series, but it is conventional to instead use the capital letters $X,$ $Y,$ and $Z$ in the context of error correction.\n"
"This convention was followed in the previous lesson, and we'll continue to do this for the remaining lessons.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang lahat ng apat na Pauli matrix ay parehong unitary at Hermitian.\n"
"Ginamit natin ang mga pangalang $\\sigma_x,$ $\\sigma_y,$ at $\\sigma_z$ para tumukoy sa mga non-identity Pauli matrix kanina sa serye, pero karaniwan na gamitin ang malalaking titik na $X,$ $Y,$ at $Z$ sa konteksto ng error correction.\n"
"Sinunod ang convention na ito sa nakaraang leksyon, at gagawin pa rin natin ito para sa natitirang mga leksyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Different non-identity Pauli matrices *anti-commute* with one another.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang iba't ibang non-identity Pauli matrix ay *anti-commute* sa isa't isa.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"XY = -YX \\qquad XZ = -ZX \\qquad YZ = -ZY\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"XY = -YX \\qquad XZ = -ZX \\qquad YZ = -ZY\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"These anti-commutation relations are simple and easy to verify by performing the multiplications, but they're critically important, in the stabilizer formalism and elsewhere.\n"
"As we will see, the minus signs that emerge when the ordering between two different non-identity Pauli matrices is reversed in a matrix product correspond precisely to the detection of errors in the stabilizer formalism.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga anti-commutation relation na ito ay simple at madaling i-verify sa pamamagitan ng pagsasagawa ng mga multiplikasyon, pero kritikal ang mga ito, sa stabilizer formalism at sa ibang lugar.\n"
"Tulad ng makikita natin, ang mga minus sign na lumalabas kapag nababago ang pagkakasunod ng dalawang magkaibang non-identity Pauli matrix sa isang matrix product ay tumutugma nang eksakto sa pagtuklas ng mga error sa stabilizer formalism.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "We also have the multiplication rules listed here.\n"
msgstr "Mayroon din tayong mga multiplication rule na nakalista dito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"XX = YY = ZZ = \\mathbb{I} \\qquad\n"
"XY = iZ \\qquad YZ = iX \\qquad ZX = iY\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"XX = YY = ZZ = \\mathbb{I} \\qquad\n"
"XY = iZ \\qquad YZ = iX \\qquad ZX = iY\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"That is, each Pauli matrix is its own inverse (which is always true for any matrix that is both unitary and Hermitian), and multiplying two different non-identity Pauli matrices together is always $\\pm i$ times the remaining non-identity Pauli matrix.\n"
"In particular, up to a phase factor, $Y$ is equivalent to $X Z,$ which explains our focus on $X$ and $Z$ errors and apparent lack of interest in $Y$ errors in quantum error correction;\n"
"$X$ represents a bit-flip, $Z$ represents a phase-flip, and so (up to a global phase factor) $Y$ represents both of those errors occurring simultaneously on the same qubit.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ibig sabihin, ang bawat Pauli matrix ay ang sarili nitong inverse (na palagi itong totoo para sa anumang matrix na parehong unitary at Hermitian), at ang pagpaparami ng dalawang magkaibang non-identity Pauli matrix ay palaging $\\pm i$ beses ng natitirang non-identity Pauli matrix.\n"
"Sa partikular, hanggang sa isang phase factor, ang $Y$ ay katumbas ng $X Z,$ na nagpapaliwanag kung bakit nakatutok tayo sa mga $X$ at $Z$ na error at tila hindi nagpapakita ng interes sa mga $Y$ na error sa quantum error correction;\n"
"Ang $X$ ay kumakatawan sa bit-flip, ang $Z$ ay kumakatawan sa phase-flip, kaya (hanggang sa isang global phase factor) ang $Y$ ay kumakatawan sa parehong mga error na nagaganap nang sabay-sabay sa iisang qubit.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli operations on multiple qubits {#pauli-operations-on-multiple-qubits}"
msgstr ""
"Mga Pauli operation sa maramihang qubit {#pauli-operations-on-multiple-"
"qubits}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The four Pauli matrices all represent operations (which could be errors) on a single qubit — and by tensoring them together we obtain operations on multiple qubits.\n"
"As a point of terminology, when we refer to an *n-qubit Pauli operation*, we mean a tensor product of any $n$ Pauli matrices, such as the examples shown here, for which $n=9.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang apat na Pauli matrix ay kumakatawan sa mga operasyon (na maaaring maging mga error) sa isang qubit — at sa pamamagitan ng pag-tensor ng mga ito nang magkasama, nagtatamo tayo ng mga operasyon sa maramihang qubit.\n"
"Bilang terminolohiya, kapag tinutukoy natin ang isang *n-qubit Pauli operation*, ibig sabihin nito ay isang tensor product ng anumang $n$ Pauli matrix, tulad ng mga halimbawang ipinapakita dito, kung saan ang $n=9.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\\\[1mm]\n"
"X \\otimes X \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\\\[1mm]\n"
"X \\otimes Y \\otimes Z \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes X \\otimes Y \\otimes Z\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\\\[1mm]\n"
"X \\otimes X \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\\\[1mm]\n"
"X \\otimes Y \\otimes Z \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes \\mathbb{I} \\otimes X \\otimes Y \\otimes Z\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Often, the term *Pauli operation* refers to a tensor product of Pauli matrices *along with a phase factor,* or sometimes just certain phase factors such as $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm i.$\n"
"There are good reasons to allow for phase factors like this from a mathematical viewpoint — but, to keep things as simple as possible, we'll use the term *Pauli operation* in this course to refer to a tensor product of Pauli matrices without the possibility of a phase factor different than 1.\n"
msgstr ""
"Madalas, ang terminong *Pauli operation* ay tumutukoy sa isang tensor product ng mga Pauli matrix *kasama ang isang phase factor,* o minsan ay ilang partikular na phase factor lamang tulad ng $\\pm 1$ at $\\pm i.$\n"
"May magagandang dahilan para payagan ang mga ganitong phase factor mula sa mathematical na pananaw — pero, para panatilihing simple ang lahat hangga't maaari, gagamitin natin ang terminong *Pauli operation* sa kursong ito para tumukoy sa isang tensor product ng mga Pauli matrix nang walang posibilidad ng phase factor na iba sa 1.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The *weight* of an $n$-qubit Pauli operation is the number of non-identity Pauli matrices in the tensor product.\n"
"For instance, the first example above has weight $0,$ the second has weight $2,$ and the third has weight $6.$\n"
"Intuitively speaking, the weight of an $n$-qubit Pauli operation is the number of qubits on which it acts non-trivially.\n"
"It's typical that quantum error correcting codes are designed so that they can detect and correct errors represented by Pauli operations so long as their weight isn't too high.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang *weight* ng isang $n$-qubit Pauli operation ay ang bilang ng mga non-identity Pauli matrix sa tensor product.\n"
"Halimbawa, ang unang halimbawa sa itaas ay may weight na $0,$ ang pangalawa ay may weight na $2,$ at ang pangatlo ay may weight na $6.$\n"
"Sa simpleng salita, ang weight ng isang $n$-qubit Pauli operation ay ang bilang ng mga qubit kung saan ito kumikilos nang hindi trivially.\n"
"Karaniwan na ang mga quantum error correcting code ay idinisenyo upang matuklas at maiwasto ang mga error na kinakatawan ng mga Pauli operation hangga't ang kanilang weight ay hindi masyadong mataas.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli operations as generators {#pauli-operations-as-generators}"
msgstr ""
"Mga Pauli operation bilang generator {#pauli-operations-as-generators}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It's sometimes useful to consider collections of Pauli operations as *generators* of sets (more specifically, *groups*) of operations, in an algebraic sense that you may recognize if you're familiar with group theory.\n"
"If you're not familiar with group theory, that's OK — it's not essential for the lesson.\n"
"A familiarity with the basics of group theory is, however, strongly recommended for those interested in exploring quantum error correction in greater depth.\n"
msgstr ""
"Minsan ay kapaki-pakinabang na isaalang-alang ang mga koleksyon ng Pauli operation bilang mga *generator* ng mga set (mas tiyak, mga *group*) ng mga operasyon, sa algebraic na kahulugan na maaaring makilala mo kung pamilyar ka sa group theory.\n"
"Kung hindi ka pamilyar sa group theory, okey lang — hindi ito mahalaga para sa leksyon.\n"
"Ang pagkakilala sa mga pangunahing kaalaman ng group theory ay, gayunpaman, lubos na inirerekomenda para sa mga interesadong mag-explore ng quantum error correction nang mas malalim.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Suppose that $P_1, \\ldots,  P_r$ are $n$-qubit Pauli operations.\n"
"When we refer to the *set generated* by $P_1, \\ldots, P_r,$ we mean the set of *all* matrices that can be obtained by multiplying these matrices together, in any combination and in any order we choose, taking each one as many times as we like.\n"
"The notation used to refer to this set is $\\langle P_1, \\ldots, P_r \\rangle.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ipagpalagay na ang $P_1, \\ldots,  P_r$ ay mga $n$-qubit Pauli operation.\n"
"Kapag tinutukoy natin ang *set na nabuo* ng $P_1, \\ldots, P_r,$ ibig sabihin nito ay ang set ng *lahat* ng matrice na maaaring makuha sa pamamagitan ng pagpaparami ng mga matrix na ito nang magkasama, sa anumang kombinasyon at sa anumang pagkakasunod na gusto natin, bawat isa ay kukuha ng maraming beses hangga't gusto natin.\n"
"Ang notasyong ginagamit para tumukoy sa set na ito ay $\\langle P_1, \\ldots, P_r \\rangle.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, the set generated by the three non-identity Pauli matrices is "
"as follows.\n"
msgstr ""
"Halimbawa, ang set na nabuo ng tatlong non-identity Pauli matrix ay "
"ganito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle X, Y, Z \\rangle = \\bigl\\{\\alpha P\\,:\\,\\alpha\\in\\{1,i,-1,-i\\},\\; P\\in\\{\\mathbb{I},X,Y,Z\\} \\bigr\\}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle X, Y, Z \\rangle = \\bigl\\{\\alpha P\\,:\\,\\alpha\\in\\{1,i,-1,-i\\},\\; P\\in\\{\\mathbb{I},X,Y,Z\\} \\bigr\\}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This can be reasoned through the multiplication rules listed earlier.\n"
"There are 16 different matrices in this set, which is commonly called the *Pauli group*.\n"
msgstr ""
"Maaari itong ma-reason sa pamamagitan ng mga multiplication rule na nakalista kanina.\n"
"May 16 na magkaibang matrix sa set na ito, na karaniwang tinatawag na *Pauli group*.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For a second example, if we remove $Y,$ we obtain half of the Pauli group.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para sa pangalawang halimbawa, kung aalisin natin ang $Y,$ makakakuha tayo "
"ng kalahati ng Pauli group.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle X, Z\\rangle = \\{ \\mathbb{I}, X, Z, -iY, -\\mathbb{I}, -X, -Z, iY \\}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle X, Z\\rangle = \\{ \\mathbb{I}, X, Z, -iY, -\\mathbb{I}, -X, -Z, iY \\}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here's one final example (for now), where this time we have $n=2.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Narito ang isang huling halimbawa (sa ngayon), kung saan sa pagkakataong ito"
" ay mayroon tayong $n=2.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle X \\otimes X, Z \\otimes Z\\rangle = \\{ \\mathbb{I}\\otimes\\mathbb{I}, X\\otimes X, Z\\otimes Z, -Y\\otimes Y \\}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle X \\otimes X, Z \\otimes Z\\rangle = \\{ \\mathbb{I}\\otimes\\mathbb{I}, X\\otimes X, Z\\otimes Z, -Y\\otimes Y \\}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this case we obtain just four elements, owing to the fact that $X\\otimes"
" X$ and $Z\\otimes Z$ commute:\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa kasong ito, nakakakuha lang tayo ng apat na elemento, dahil nagco-commute"
" ang $X\\otimes X$ at $Z\\otimes Z$:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"(X\\otimes X)(Z\\otimes Z) & = (XZ) \\otimes (XZ)\\\\\n"
"& = (-ZX)\\otimes (-ZX)\\\\\n"
"& = (ZX)\\otimes (ZX)\\\\\n"
"& = (Z\\otimes Z)(X\\otimes X).\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"(X\\otimes X)(Z\\otimes Z) & = (XZ) \\otimes (XZ)\\\\\n"
"& = (-ZX)\\otimes (-ZX)\\\\\n"
"& = (ZX)\\otimes (ZX)\\\\\n"
"& = (Z\\otimes Z)(X\\otimes X).\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli observables {#pauli-observables}"
msgstr "Mga Pauli observable {#pauli-observables}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli matrices, and $n$-qubit Pauli operations more generally, are unitary, and therefore they describe unitary operations on qubits.\n"
"But they're also *Hermitian* matrices, and for this reason they describe *measurements,* as will now be explained.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga Pauli matrix, at ang mga $n$-qubit Pauli operation sa pangkalahatan, ay unitary, kaya isinasalarawan nila ang mga unitary na operasyon sa mga qubit.\n"
"Pero sila rin ay mga *Hermitian* matrix, at sa kadahilanang ito ay nagsasalarawan sila ng mga *sukat*, tulad ng ipapaliwanag ngayon.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hermitian matrix observables {#hermitian-matrix-observables}"
msgstr "Mga observable na Hermitian matrix {#hermitian-matrix-observables}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consider first an arbitrary Hermitian matrix $A.$\n"
"When we refer to $A$ as an *observable*, we're associating with $A$ a certain uniquely defined projective measurement.\n"
"In words, the possible outcomes are the distinct eigenvalues of $A,$ and the projections that define the measurement are the ones that project onto the spaces spanned by the corresponding eigenvectors of $A.$\n"
"So, the outcomes for such a measurement happen to be real numbers — but because matrices have only finitely many eigenvalues, there will only be finitely many different measurement outcomes for a given choice of $A.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Isaalang-alang muna ang isang arbitrary na Hermitian matrix $A.$\n"
"Kapag tinutukoy natin ang $A$ bilang isang *observable*, nag-uugnay tayo sa $A$ ng isang tiyak na natatanging projective measurement.\n"
"Sa mga salita, ang mga posibleng kinalabasan ay ang mga natatanging eigenvalue ng $A,$ at ang mga projection na nagbibigay-kahulugan sa sukat ay ang mga nagpo-project sa mga espasyo na sinasaklaw ng mga kaukulang eigenvector ng $A.$\n"
"Kaya, ang mga kinalabasan para sa ganitong sukat ay maaaring maging mga tunay na numero — pero dahil ang mga matrix ay mayroon lamang natapos na bilang ng mga eigenvalue, magkakaroon lamang ng natapos na bilang ng iba't ibang kinalabasan ng sukat para sa isang partikular na pagpili ng $A.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In greater detail, by the spectral theorem, it is possible to write\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa mas detalyadong paraan, ayon sa spectral theorem, posibleng isulat ang\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"A = \\sum_{k = 1}^m \\lambda_k \\Pi_k\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"A = \\sum_{k = 1}^m \\lambda_k \\Pi_k\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for *distinct* real number eigenvalues $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$ and "
"projections $\\Pi_1,\\ldots,\\Pi_m$ satisfying\n"
msgstr ""
"para sa mga *natatanging* tunay na eigenvalue na "
"$\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$ at mga projection na $\\Pi_1,\\ldots,\\Pi_m$"
" na tumutugon sa\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\Pi_1 + \\cdots + \\Pi_m = \\mathbb{I}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\Pi_1 + \\cdots + \\Pi_m = \\mathbb{I}.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Such an expression of a matrix is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues.\n"
"Another way to say this is that, if we insist that the eigenvalues are ordered in decreasing value $\\lambda_1 > \\lambda_2 > \\cdots > \\lambda_m,$ then there's only one way to write $A$ in the form above.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang ganitong expression ng isang matrix ay natatangi hanggang sa pagkakasunod ng mga eigenvalue.\n"
"Isa pang paraan ng pagsasabi nito ay, kung iginigiit natin na ang mga eigenvalue ay nakaayos sa bumababang halaga na $\\lambda_1 > \\lambda_2 > \\cdots > \\lambda_m,$ may isang paraan lamang ng pagsulat ng $A$ sa form sa itaas.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Based on this expression, the measurement we associate with the observable "
"$A$ is the projective measurement described by the projections "
"$\\Pi_1,\\ldots,\\Pi_m,$ and the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$"
" are understood to be the measurement outcomes corresponding to these "
"projections.\n"
msgstr ""
"Batay sa expression na ito, ang sukat na inuugnay natin sa observable na $A$"
" ay ang projective measurement na inilarawan ng mga projection na "
"$\\Pi_1,\\ldots,\\Pi_m,$ at ang mga eigenvalue na "
"$\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$ ay naiintindihang mga kinalabasan ng sukat "
"na naaayon sa mga projection na ito.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measurements from Pauli operations {#measurements-from-pauli-operations}"
msgstr ""
"Mga sukat mula sa Pauli operation {#measurements-from-pauli-operations}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's see what measurements of the sort just described look like for Pauli operations, starting with the three non-identity Pauli matrices.\n"
"These matrices have spectral decompositions as follows.\n"
msgstr ""
"Tingnan natin kung ano ang hitsura ng mga sukat ng ganitong uri para sa mga Pauli operation, simula sa tatlong non-identity Pauli matrix.\n"
"Ang mga matrix na ito ay may mga spectral decomposition tulad ng sumusunod.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"X = \\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert - \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert\\\\\n"
"Y = \\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert - \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert\\\\\n"
"Z = \\vert {0} \\rangle\\langle {0} \\vert - \\vert {1} \\rangle\\langle {1} \\vert\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"X = \\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert - \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert\\\\\n"
"Y = \\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert - \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert\\\\\n"
"Z = \\vert {0} \\rangle\\langle {0} \\vert - \\vert {1} \\rangle\\langle {1} \\vert\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The measurements defined by $X,$ $Y,$ and $Z,$ viewed as observables, are therefore the projective measurements defined by the following\n"
"sets of projections, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga sukat na tinukoy ng $X,$ $Y,$ at $Z,$ na itinuturing bilang mga observable, ay samakatuwid ang mga projective measurement na tinukoy ng mga sumusunod na\n"
"set ng mga projection, ayon sa pagkakasunod.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\bigl\\{\\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert, \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert \\bigr\\} \\\\\n"
"\\bigl\\{\\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert, \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert\\bigr\\} \\\\\n"
"\\bigl\\{\\vert {0} \\rangle\\langle {0} \\vert, \\vert {1} \\rangle\\langle {1} \\vert\\bigr\\}\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\bigl\\{\\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert, \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert \\bigr\\} \\\\\n"
"\\bigl\\{\\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert, \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert\\bigr\\} \\\\\n"
"\\bigl\\{\\vert {0} \\rangle\\langle {0} \\vert, \\vert {1} \\rangle\\langle {1} \\vert\\bigr\\}\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In all three cases, the two possible measurement outcomes are the eigenvalues $+1$ and $-1.$\n"
"Such measurements are commonly called $X$-measurements, $Y$-measurements, and $Z$-measurements.\n"
"We encountered these measurements in the \"General measurements\" lesson of \"General formulation of quantum information,\"\n"
"where they arose in the context of quantum state tomography.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa lahat ng tatlong kaso, ang dalawang posibleng kinalabasan ng sukat ay ang mga eigenvalue na $+1$ at $-1.$\n"
"Ang mga ganitong sukat ay karaniwang tinatawag na $X$-measurement, $Y$-measurement, at $Z$-measurement.\n"
"Naranasan natin ang mga sukat na ito sa leksyong \"General measurements\" ng \"General formulation of quantum information,\"\n"
"kung saan lumitaw ang mga ito sa konteksto ng quantum state tomography.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Of course, a $Z$-measurement is essentially just a standard basis "
"measurement and an $X$ measurement is a measurement with respect to the "
"plus/minus basis of a qubit — but, as these measurements are described here,"
" we're taking the eigenvalues $+1$ and $-1$ to be the actual measurement "
"outcomes.\n"
msgstr ""
"Siyempre, ang $Z$-measurement ay mahalagang isang standard basis measurement"
" at ang $X$ measurement ay isang sukat na may kaugnayan sa plus/minus basis "
"ng isang qubit — pero, tulad ng inilarawan dito ang mga sukat na ito, "
"tinatanggap natin ang mga eigenvalue na $+1$ at $-1$ bilang aktwal na mga "
"kinalabasan ng sukat.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The same prescription can be followed for Pauli operations on $n\\geq 2$ qubits, though it must be stressed that there will still be just two possible outcomes for the measurements described in this way: $+1$ and $-1,$ which are the only possible eigenvalues of Pauli operations.\n"
"The two corresponding projections will therefore have rank higher than one in this case.\n"
"More precisely, for every non-identity $n$-qubit Pauli operation, the $2^n$ dimensional state space always splits into two subspaces of eigenvectors having equal dimension, so the two projections that define the associated measurement will both have rank $2^{n-1}.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang parehong pamamaraan ay maaaring sundin para sa mga Pauli operation sa $n\\geq 2$ qubit, bagama't dapat igiit na mayroon pa ring dalawang posibleng kinalabasan lamang para sa mga sukat na inilarawan sa ganitong paraan: $+1$ at $-1,$ na siyang tanging posibleng eigenvalue ng mga Pauli operation.\n"
"Ang dalawang kaukulang projection ay magkakaroon ng rank na mas mataas sa isa sa kasong ito.\n"
"Mas tiyak, para sa bawat non-identity na $n$-qubit Pauli operation, ang $2^n$ dimensional na state space ay palaging nahahati sa dalawang subspace ng mga eigenvector na may pantay na dimensyon, kaya ang dalawang projection na nagbibigay-kahulugan sa kaukulang sukat ay magkakaroon ng rank na $2^{n-1}.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The measurement described by an $n$-qubit Pauli operation, considered as an observable, is therefore not the same thing as a measurement with respect to an *orthonormal basis* of eigenvectors of that operation, nor is it the same thing as independently measuring each of the corresponding Pauli matrices independently, as observables, on $n$ qubits.\n"
"Both of those alternatives would necessitate $2^n$ possible measurement outcomes, but here we have just the two possible outcomes $+1$ and $-1.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang sukat na inilarawan ng isang $n$-qubit Pauli operation, itinuturing bilang isang observable, ay samakatuwid ay hindi katulad ng isang sukat na may kaugnayan sa isang *orthonormal basis* ng mga eigenvector ng operasyong iyon, ni hindi rin katulad ng independiyenteng pagsukat ng bawat isa sa mga kaukulang Pauli matrix nang independiyente, bilang mga observable, sa $n$ qubit.\n"
"Ang parehong mga alternatibo ay mangangailangan ng $2^n$ posibleng kinalabasan ng sukat, pero dito ay mayroon lamang tayong dalawang posibleng kinalabasan na $+1$ at $-1.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, consider the 2-qubit Pauli operation $Z\\otimes Z$ as an observable.\n"
"We can effectively take the tensor product of the spectral decompositions to obtain one for the tensor product.\n"
msgstr ""
"Halimbawa, isaalang-alang ang 2-qubit Pauli operation na $Z\\otimes Z$ bilang isang observable.\n"
"Maaari tayong epektibong kumuha ng tensor product ng mga spectral decomposition para makakuha ng isa para sa tensor product.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"Z\\otimes Z & = (\\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert - \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert) \\otimes (\\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert - \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert)\\\\\n"
"& = \\bigl( \\vert 00\\rangle\\langle 00\\vert + \\vert 11\\rangle\\langle 11\\vert \\bigr) - \\bigl( \\vert 01\\rangle\\langle 01\\vert + \\vert 10\\rangle\\langle 10\\vert \\bigr)\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"Z\\otimes Z & = (\\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert - \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert) \\otimes (\\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert - \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert)\\\\\n"
"& = \\bigl( \\vert 00\\rangle\\langle 00\\vert + \\vert 11\\rangle\\langle 11\\vert \\bigr) - \\bigl( \\vert 01\\rangle\\langle 01\\vert + \\vert 10\\rangle\\langle 10\\vert \\bigr)\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That is, we have $Z\\otimes Z = \\Pi_0 - \\Pi_1$ for\n"
msgstr "Ibig sabihin, mayroon tayong $Z\\otimes Z = \\Pi_0 - \\Pi_1$ para sa\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\Pi_0 = \\vert 00\\rangle\\langle 00\\vert + \\vert 11\\rangle\\langle 11\\vert\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\Pi_1 = \\vert 01\\rangle\\langle 01\\vert + \\vert 10\\rangle\\langle 10\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\Pi_0 = \\vert 00\\rangle\\langle 00\\vert + \\vert 11\\rangle\\langle 11\\vert\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\Pi_1 = \\vert 01\\rangle\\langle 01\\vert + \\vert 10\\rangle\\langle 10\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"so these are the two projections that define the measurement.\n"
"If, for instance, we were to measure a $\\vert\\phi^+\\rangle$ Bell state nondestructively using this measurement, then we would be certain to obtain the outcome $+1,$ and the state would be unchanged as a result of the measurement.\n"
"In particular, the state would not collapse to $\\vert 00\\rangle$ or $\\vert 11\\rangle.$\n"
msgstr ""
"kaya ito ang dalawang projection na nagbibigay-kahulugan sa sukat.\n"
"Kung, halimbawa, susukatin natin ang isang $\\vert\\phi^+\\rangle$ Bell state nang hindi nagsira gamit ang sukat na ito, magiging sigurado tayo na makukuha ang kinalabasan na $+1,$ at ang estado ay mananatiling hindi nagbago dahil sa sukat.\n"
"Sa partikular, ang estado ay hindi mag-co-collapse sa $\\vert 00\\rangle$ o $\\vert 11\\rangle.$\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Nondestructive implementation through phase estimation {#nondestructive-"
"implementation-through-phase-estimation}"
msgstr ""
"Nondestructive na implementasyon sa pamamagitan ng phase estimation "
"{#nondestructive-implementation-through-phase-estimation}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For any $n$-qubit Pauli operation, we can perform the measurement associated"
" with that observable nondestructively using phase estimation.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para sa anumang $n$-qubit Pauli operation, maaari nating isagawa ang sukat "
"na nauugnay sa observable na iyon nang hindi nagsisira gamit ang phase "
"estimation.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here's a circuit based on phase estimation that works for any Pauli matrix $P,$ where the measurement is being performed on the top qubit.\n"
"The outcomes $0$ and $1$ of the standard basis measurement in the circuit correspond to the eigenvalues $+1$ and $-1,$ just like we usually have for phase estimation with one control qubit.\n"
"(Note that the control qubit is on the bottom in this diagram, whereas in the \"Phase estimation and factoring\"\n"
"lesson of \"Fundamentals of quantum algorithms\" the control qubits were drawn on the top.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Narito ang isang circuit batay sa phase estimation na gumagana para sa anumang Pauli matrix $P,$ kung saan ang sukat ay isinasagawa sa itaas na qubit.\n"
"Ang mga kinalabasan na $0$ at $1$ ng standard basis measurement sa circuit ay naaayon sa mga eigenvalue na $+1$ at $-1,$ tulad ng karaniwang mayroon tayo para sa phase estimation na may isang control qubit.\n"
"(Tandaan na ang control qubit ay nasa ibaba sa diagram na ito, samantalang sa leksyong \"Phase estimation and factoring\"\n"
"ng \"Fundamentals of quantum algorithms\" ang mga control qubit ay iginuhit sa itaas.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Circuit for measuring a Pauli observable using phase "
"estimation](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/phase-estimation-Pauli.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![circuit for measuring a Pauli observable using phase "
"estimation](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/phase-estimation-Pauli.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A similar method works for Pauli operations on multiple qubits.\n"
"For example, the following circuit diagram illustrates a nondestructive measurement of the $3$-qubit Pauli observable $P_2\\otimes P_1\\otimes P_0,$ for any choice of $P_0,P_1,P_2 \\in \\{X,Y,Z\\}.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang katulad na pamamaraan ay gumagana para sa mga Pauli operation sa maramihang qubit.\n"
"Halimbawa, ang sumusunod na circuit diagram ay naglalarawan ng isang nondestructive measurement ng $3$-qubit Pauli observable na $P_2\\otimes P_1\\otimes P_0,$ para sa anumang pagpili ng $P_0,P_1,P_2 \\in \\{X,Y,Z\\}.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Circuit for measuring a 3-qubit Pauli observable using phase "
"estimation](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/phase-estimation-3-qubit-Pauli.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![circuit for measuring a 3-qubit Pauli observable using phase "
"estimation](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/phase-estimation-3-qubit-Pauli.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This approach generalizes to $n$-qubit Pauli observables, for any $n,$ in the natural way.\n"
"Of course, we only need to include controlled-unitary gates for *non-identity* tensor factors of Pauli observables when implementing such measurements; controlled-identity gates are simply identity gates and can therefore be omitted.\n"
"This means that lower weight Pauli observables require smaller circuits to be implemented through this approach.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang diskarteng ito ay nagge-generalize sa mga $n$-qubit Pauli observable, para sa anumang $n,$ sa natural na paraan.\n"
"Siyempre, kailangan lamang nating isama ang mga controlled-unitary gate para sa mga *non-identity* tensor factor ng mga Pauli observable kapag isinasagawa ang mga ganitong sukat; ang mga controlled-identity gate ay simpleng mga identity gate at maaaring kaya ay aalisin.\n"
"Ibig sabihin nito na ang mas mababang weight na Pauli observable ay nangangailangan ng mas maliliit na circuit na ipatupad sa pamamagitan ng diskarteng ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Notice that, irrespective of $n,$ these phase-estimation circuits have just a single control qubit, which is consistent with the fact that there are just two possible measurement outcomes for these measurements.\n"
"Using more control qubits wouldn't reveal additional information because these measurements are already perfect using a single control qubit.\n"
"(One way to see this is directly from the general procedure for phase estimation: the assumption $U^2 = \\mathbb{I}$ renders any additional control qubits beyond the first pointless.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Pansinin na, anuman ang $n,$ ang mga phase-estimation circuit na ito ay may isang control qubit lamang, na naaayon sa katotohanan na mayroon lamang dalawang posibleng kinalabasan ng sukat para sa mga sukat na ito.\n"
"Ang paggamit ng mas maraming control qubit ay hindi magpapahayag ng karagdagang impormasyon dahil perpekto na ang mga sukat na ito gamit ang isang control qubit.\n"
"(Isang paraan upang makita ito ay direkta mula sa pangkalahatang pamamaraan para sa phase estimation: ang assumption na $U^2 = \\mathbb{I}$ ay nagpapawalang-saysay sa anumang karagdagang control qubit na higit sa una.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here's a specific example, of a nondestructive implementation of a "
"$Z\\otimes Z$ measurement, which is relevant to the description of the 3-bit"
" repetition code as a stabilizer code that we'll see shortly.\n"
msgstr ""
"Narito ang isang partikular na halimbawa, ng isang nondestructive na "
"implementasyon ng isang $Z\\otimes Z$ measurement, na may kaugnayan sa "
"paglalarawan ng 3-bit repetition code bilang isang stabilizer code na "
"makikita natin sa lalong madaling panahon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Circuit for measuring a ZZ observable using phase "
"estimation](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/phase-estimation-ZZ.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![circuit for measuring a ZZ observable using phase "
"estimation](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/phase-estimation-ZZ.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this case, and for tensor products of more than two $Z$ observables more "
"generally, the circuit can be simplified.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa kasong ito, at para sa mga tensor product ng higit sa dalawang $Z$ "
"observable sa pangkalahatan, ang circuit ay maaaring pasimplehin.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Simplified circuit for measuring a ZZ "
"observable](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/simplified-ZZ.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![Simplified circuit for measuring a ZZ "
"observable](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-"
"correction/stabilizer-formalism/simplified-ZZ.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Thus, this measurement is equivalent to nondestructively measuring the "
"parity (or XOR) of the standard basis states of two qubits.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kaya, ang sukat na ito ay katumbas ng nondestructive na pagsukat ng parity "
"(o XOR) ng mga standard basis state ng dalawang qubit.\n"
